// A window can be given another size by the user (`Window.resizable`),
// and fills the screen while `Window.fullscreen` says so: `Window.width`
// and `Window.height` are what it is, every frame, and what a program
// lays out for them follows. With the window device that has no window
// (it is "dragged" 100 wider and 60 higher in its third frame, and 1280
// by 720 while it fills the screen, and as big as the program asks).
// RUN: sh %S/Inputs/headless.sh %t.dir
// RUN: %ent run -I %t.dir -I %S/../../examples %S/Inputs/window_resize.ent \
// RUN:     2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s

// As it was opened: 400 less the bar, the padding and the gap.
// CHECK:      1: window 400 x 300, the rest 270.0 x 280.0 at 120.0
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: window 400 x 300, the rest 270.0 x 280.0 at 120.0
// Another size.
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: window 500 x 360, the rest 370.0 x 340.0 at 120.0
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: window 500 x 360, the rest 370.0 x 340.0 at 120.0
// The screen: asked for in a frame, it is so in the next.
// CHECK-NEXT: 5: window 500 x 360, the rest 370.0 x 340.0 at 120.0
// CHECK-NEXT: 6: window 1280 x 720, the rest 1150.0 x 700.0 at 120.0
// Everything twice as big (`Window.zoom`): half as many points to lay
// out for, in the window as it was before it filled the screen.
// CHECK-NEXT: 7: window 250 x 180, the rest 120.0 x 160.0 at 120.0
// The program gave it another size (`resize_window`), 600 by 400.
// CHECK-NEXT: 8: window 300 x 200, the rest 170.0 x 180.0 at 120.0
